// `merge` over any number of maps, 2026-10-22. It **replaced** a two-parameter form rather than
// joining it: a variadic `Map<K, V>` accepts exactly two maps as well, so the pair is
// indistinguishable and the declaration checker refuses it -- the same wall `remove_all`'s `Set<K>`
// form hit, answered the other way because here the variadic *subsumes* the binary form instead of
// meaning something different from it. Every `merge(a, b)` call site is unchanged; only a named
// call could notice, the parameters having gone from `m`/`other` to `maps`.
//
// A later map wins where an earlier one has the same key, and keys keep the order they were first
// seen in -- which is why reversing the arguments changes both the values and the order.
fn main() {
    val a = Map<string, int> { "a": 1, "b": 2 }
    val b = Map<string, int> { "b": 20, "c": 3 }
    val c = Map<string, int> { "c": 30, "d": 4 }

    print(merge(a, b))
    print(merge(a, b, c))
    // reversed: `a` now wins on "b", `b` on "c", and the key order follows the first map seen
    print(merge(c, b, a))
    // one map answers that map
    print(merge(a))
    print(merge(Map<string, int> {}))

    // **One argument that is an array of maps spreads**, as `concat` and `push` do
    val all: [Map<string, int>] = [a, b, c]
    print(merge(all))
    // named, and a type argument
    print(merge(maps: all))
    print(merge<string, int>(a, b))

    // every source untouched
    print(a)
    print(b)

    // **The two in-place members are variadic on their second parameter too**, one taking a tail
    // of `Map`s and the other a tail of `MutableMap`s. `#[at_least_one]` on both is load-bearing
    // rather than merely sensible: without it `merge(mb)` passes an empty tail that fits both and
    // the declaration checker refuses the pair outright. With it the floor is two arguments, where
    // position one is a `Map` against a `MutableMap` and disjoint.
    val mb = mutable(a)
    merge(mb, b)
    print(snapshot(mb))
    merge(mb, b, c)
    print(snapshot(mb))
    // the tail spreads from a lone array here as well
    merge(mb, all)
    print(snapshot(mb))
    // and the MutableMap-tail declaration, one and several
    val b1 = mutable(Map<string, int> { "f": 6 })
    val b2 = mutable(Map<string, int> { "g": 7 })
    merge(mb, b1)
    merge(mb, b1, b2)
    print(snapshot(mb))
    // named
    merge(m: mb, entries: all)
    print(len(snapshot(mb)))
}
